$DIR/main.p:5:39: cannot pass &n to yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:8), which keeps it after returning
    &n is a pointer to n. n is a parameter of ptrs (declared at main.p:4). It
    lives in ptrs's frame, like a local variable. yield (which may call the loop
    body at main.p:8) stores p in package variable saved (main.p:9), so &n would
    outlive n.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:8) should keep its own copy
    of n, change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep:
    an owned[int] from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
